The files are:
1. ManagementGroup/Generated/LongRunningOperation/ManagementGroupsArmOperation.cs (line 61)
2. ManagementGroup/Generated/LongRunningOperation/ManagementGroupsArmOperationOfT.cs (line 61)
3. Resources/Generated/LongRunningOperation/ResourcesArmOperation.cs (line 61)
4. Resources/Generated/LongRunningOperation/ResourcesArmOperationOfT.cs (line 61)

Each has this pattern:
```csharp
var lroDetails = ModelReaderWriter.Write(rehydrationToken, ModelReaderWriterOptions.Json, AzureResourceManagerContext.Default).ToObjectFromJson<Dictionary<string, string>>();
return lroDetails["id"];
```

`ToObjectFromJson<T>()` internally calls `JsonSerializer.Deserialize<T>()` which is not AOT-safe.

Replace the pattern with parsing the JSON manually to extract the "id" field using JsonDocument:
```csharp
var lroData = ModelReaderWriter.Write(rehydrationToken, ModelReaderWriterOptions.Json, AzureResourceManagerContext.Default);
using var lroDoc = JsonDocument.Parse(lroData);
return lroDoc.RootElement.GetProperty("id").GetString();
```

For each file, use the edit tool to replace the GetOperationId method body. The current method looks like:
```csharp
private string GetOperationId(RehydrationToken? rehydrationToken)
{
    if (rehydrationToken is null)
    {
        return null;
    }
    var lroDetails = ModelReaderWriter.Write(rehydrationToken, ModelReaderWriterOptions.Json, AzureResourceManagerContext.Default).ToObjectFromJson<Dictionary<string, string>>();
    return lroDetails["id"];
}
```

Replace the method body (after the null check) with:
```csharp
private string GetOperationId(RehydrationToken? rehydrationToken)
{
    if (rehydrationToken is null)
    {
        return null;
    }
    var lroData = ModelReaderWriter.Write(rehydrationToken, ModelReaderWriterOptions.Json, AzureResourceManagerContext.Default);
    using var lroDoc = JsonDocument.Parse(lroData);
    return lroDoc.RootElement.GetProperty("id").GetString();
}
```

Also make sure `System.Text.Json` is in the using directives (add `using System.Text.Json;` if not present). Remove `using System.Collections.Generic;` if `Dictionary` is no longer used (but check if it's used elsewhere in the file first - it probably is NOT used elsewhere in these files so remove it).

